# AUTHORED-BY Claude Fable 5
#
# Machine-readable normative-statement companion for "Redirect-Free WebAuthn
# Re-Authentication for Solid-OIDC" (https://jeswr.github.io/solid-webauthn-reauth-spec/).
# COMPLEMENTARY: the full-text spec is the normative document and is unchanged; this
# graph enumerates its normative statements so tooling (conformance suites, the interop
# map, coverage gates) can consume them.
#
# Model: the Solid Protocol's own requirement markup (http://www.w3.org/ns/spec#) as a
# sidecar graph, + the certification testability spine (E / A-int / A-exist / P), +
# honest per-statement test-gap accounting — the jeswr/spec-companion format
# (shapes/spec-companion.shape.ttl + tools/validate.mjs in that repo). Statement texts
# are VERBATIM quotes of the spec source (validator-enforced against the pinned
# index.html — tags stripped, entities decoded, [[refs]] unbracketed, whitespace
# collapsed).
#
# Validate (from a sibling jeswr/spec-companion checkout):
#   node ../spec-companion/tools/validate.mjs spec.statements.ttl --spec-html index.html
#
# FORMAL-MODEL SEAM (first sc:formalModel candidate in the spec family): this spec is a
# protocol/state-machine security core — the challenge lifecycle (issue -> outstanding ->
# atomically-consumed exactly once), the consume-BEFORE-verify ordering, the
# DPoP-failure-does-not-consume exception, credential revocation, and the clustered
# atomicity requirement together form a small state machine whose safety properties
# (no challenge redeemed twice; no assertion replayable after any outcome; no
# consumption on a DPoP-rejected request) are exactly what a TLA+/Alloy model pins
# cheaply and black-box vectors cannot (race interleavings). The candidate statements
# are marked "sc:formalModel seam candidate" in their rdfs:comment below:
# SWRA-VER-1, SWRA-VER-2, SWRA-VER-4, SWRA-CHAL-1, SWRA-CHAL-2, SWRA-CHAL-3,
# SWRA-CHAL-5, SWRA-CHAL-6, SWRA-CRED-4. No model exists yet, so no sc:formalModel
# link is asserted (the property takes an IRI to a machine-checked model).
#
# Canonical published home: beside the spec, as
# https://jeswr.github.io/solid-webauthn-reauth-spec/spec.statements.ttl (the @base).

@base <https://jeswr.github.io/solid-webauthn-reauth-spec/spec.statements.ttl> .
@prefix sc:      <https://w3id.org/jeswr/spec-companion#> .
@prefix spec:    <http://www.w3.org/ns/spec#> .
@prefix dcterms: <http://purl.org/dc/terms/> .
@prefix prov:    <http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#> .
@prefix rdfs:    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix xsd:     <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .

#################################################################
# The companion document
#################################################################

<> a sc:CompanionDocument ;
    dcterms:title "Redirect-Free WebAuthn Re-Authentication for Solid-OIDC — normative-statement companion"@en ;
    sc:companionOf <https://jeswr.github.io/solid-webauthn-reauth-spec/> ;
    sc:specVersion "1c9dde919b96e8058ddb59529dd3059d5d310310" ;
    dcterms:created "2026-07-06"^^xsd:date ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo <https://jeswr.org/#me> ;
    dcterms:description "Extracted with AI assistance (Claude Fable 5, Anthropic) from the pinned spec source; awaiting review by the human editor, like the spec itself."@en ;
    sc:extractionNote "The five registration-options bullets (#registration-options) — including the req-marked req-uv-reg (userVerification = \"required\") and req-resident-key (residentKey = \"required\") — carry no BCP 14 keyword; they are normative only via the lead-in 'MUST return … carrying:'. Captured as SWRA-REG-1. Errata candidate: give req-uv-reg and req-resident-key their own MUSTs." ;
    sc:extractionNote "The five registration-verification steps (#registration-verify) are normative only via the lead-in 'the OpenID Provider MUST, in order:' and carry no per-step BCP 14 keyword. Captured as SWRA-REG-4. Errata candidate." ;
    sc:extractionNote "Verification steps 1–6 (#verification) — including the req-marked req-no-embed (embedding rejection), req-challenge-consume (atomic single-use consumption), and req-signcount (strictly-increasing counter) — carry no per-step BCP 14 keyword except req-uv's embedded MUST; they are normative only via the lead-in 'MUST verify the relayed assertion in the following order'. Captured as SWRA-VER-1 and SWRA-VER-5. Errata candidate: give req-no-embed, req-challenge-consume, and req-signcount their own keywords." ;
    sc:extractionNote "The token-exchange request-parameter enumeration (#token-exchange, 'Re-authentication is an OAuth 2.0 Token Exchange … with:') carries no BCP 14 keyword; conformance to the request shape hangs on the profile definition and the OP-side rejections (SWRA-BUNDLE-1..6, SWRA-TX-1). Errata candidate." ;
    sc:extractionNote "'Credentials MUST never appear in fetched URLs or logs.' (#security-ssrf) uses 'MUST never' — a non-BCP14 negation form (RFC 8174 reserves MUST NOT). Captured as SWRA-SEC-SSRF-2 at the literal keyword level MUST. Errata candidate: rephrase as MUST NOT." ;
    sc:extractionNote "Five sentences mix two requirement levels and are split here: req-client-downgrade (MUST + MUST NOT → SWRA-CLIENT-1/-2), the key-hygiene sentence of req-single-keypair (SHOULD + MUST NOT → SWRA-CLIENT-4/-5), the credential-listing sentence (MUST + MUST NOT → SWRA-CRED-2/-3), the lifetime/revocation sentence (SHOULD + MAY → SWRA-CRED-5/-6), and the precise-reason sentence of req-coarse-error (MUST NOT + MAY → SWRA-ERR-2/-3)." ;
    sc:extractionNote "Future-profile / change-controller MAYs are NOT extracted — they bind no conformance class of this version: accepting crossOrigin == true against a topOrigin allow-list (#security-embedding), IANA registration of an urn:ietf token type (#registrations), and future bundle-version assignment (#reg-bundle-version). The note-8693-tension MUST (#errors) is likewise not extracted: it is a quotation of RFC 8693 §2.2.2's directive, not a requirement of this specification." .

